# Python Development Standards for NetGrid

## Code Style and Quality
- Follow PEP 8 style guidelines strictly
- Use Python 3.8+ features and type hints for all function parameters and return values
- Write comprehensive docstrings for all public APIs using Google-style format
- Keep functions focused and single-purpose (max 20-30 lines)
- Use meaningful variable and function names that reflect their purpose

## Architecture Principles
- Maintain modular architecture with clear separation of concerns
- Keep core business logic in `src/netgrid/core/`
- Place display/formatting logic in `src/netgrid/display/`
- Put utility functions in `src/netgrid/utils/`
- CLI interface should be thin and delegate to core modules

## Testing Requirements
- Write unit tests for all new functionality
- Aim for 90%+ test coverage
- Test both happy path and edge cases
- Use descriptive test names that explain what is being tested
- Mock external dependencies (system calls, network requests)

## Import Organization
- Group imports: standard library, third-party, local
- Use absolute imports for project modules
- Avoid circular imports between modules
- Keep imports at the top of the file

## Error Handling
- Use specific exception types rather than generic Exception
- Provide meaningful error messages
- Log errors appropriately for debugging
- Handle edge cases gracefully (e.g., network interfaces that disappear)

## Performance Considerations
- Optimize for sub-second response time
- Cache expensive operations (vendor lookups, system queries)
- Use efficient data structures for large datasets
- Consider memory usage for real-time monitoring scenarios
